# ###################
# Docs: Loops
# ###################
[import = Docs: "@/layouts/Docs.arcm" !]
[import = Code: "@/components/Code.arcm" !]
[import = Playground: "@/components/Playground.arcm" !]
[import = hl: "@/components/docs/Hl.arcm" !]
[import = Text: "@/components/docs/Text.arcm" !]
[import = Title: "@/components/docs/Title.arcm" !]
[import = Link: "@/components/docs/Link.arcm" !]
[import = List: "@/components/docs/List.arcm" !]
[import = Item: "@/components/docs/Item.arcm" !]

[Docs = slug: "loops"]
  [Title = id: "for-each"]\[for-each][end:Title]
  [Text][hl = text: "[for-each]" !] writes its body once for each item of a list. Try it: add a name and press Run:[end:Text]
  [Playground = title: "index.arcm", text: '
    ${ const names = ["Adam", "Eid", "Ismail"]; }$
    [ul]
      [for-each = ${ names }$, as: "name"]
        [li]${ name }$[end]
      [end]
    [end]
  ' !]
  [List]
    [Item]The list comes from [hl = text: "${ }$" !] and is read when the page is built.[end:Item]
    [Item][hl = text: "as" !] names the item. Without it, the item is called [hl = text: "value" !].[end:Item]
    [Item]For a list that changes in the browser, use a [Link = text: "live loop", href: "#live" !].[end:Item]
  [end:List]

  [Title = id: "index"]The index: i[end:Title]
  [Text][hl = text: "i" !] is the item's position, starting at 0:[end:Text]
  [Code = output: true, text: '
    [for-each = ${ ["Gold", "Silver", "Bronze"] }$]
      [p = data-place: ${ i }$]${ i + 1 }$. ${ value }$[end]
    [end]
  ' !]

  [Title = id: "objects"]Lists of objects[end:Title]
  [Text]Each item can be an object. Use its fields in text and in props:[end:Text]
  [Code = output: true, text: '
    ${ const users = [{ name: "Adam", role: "admin" }, { name: "Elmi", role: "editor" }]; }$
    [for-each = ${ users }$, as: "user"]
      [p = class: ${ user.role }$]${ user.name }$[end]
    [end]
  ' !]

  [Title = id: "from-file"]Data from a file[end:Title]
  [Text]Import a JSON file in [hl = text: "${ }$" !] and loop over it:[end:Text]
  [Code = lang: "json", title: "posts.json", file: "src/samples/loops/posts.json" !]
  [Code = title: "index.arcm", output: true, file: "src/samples/loops/from-file.arcm" !]

  [Title = id: "nested"]Nested loops[end:Title]
  [Text]Loops can go inside loops. The inner [hl = text: "i" !] hides the outer one, so keep the outer index in a [hl = text: "const" !] first:[end:Text]
  [Code = output: true, text: '
    ${ const rows = [["a", "b"], ["c"]]; }$
    [for-each = ${ rows }$, as: "row"]
      ${ const r = i; }$
      [for-each = ${ row }$, as: "cell"]
        [span]${ r }$.${ i }$ ${ cell }$[end]
      [end]
    [end]
  ' !]

  [Title = id: "iterables"]What you can loop over[end:Title]
  [List]
    [Item]Arrays, strings, [hl = text: "Set" !] and [hl = text: "Map" !]: anything JavaScript can loop over with [hl = text: "for…of" !].[end:Item]
    [Item]An object: use [hl = text: "Object.entries(obj)" !], then [hl = text: "entry[0]" !] is the key and [hl = text: "entry[1]" !] the value.[end:Item]
    [Item]A number of times: [hl = text: "Array.from({ length: 3 }, (_, n) => n + 1)" !] gives [hl = text: "1, 2, 3" !].[end:Item]
    [Item]An empty list writes nothing. A plain object or a number writes nothing too, with a warning that says how to loop over it.[end:Item]
  [end:List]
  [Code = output: true, text: '
    ${ const prices = { tea: 2, cake: 4 }; }$
    [for-each = ${ Object.entries(prices) }$, as: "entry"]
      [p]${ entry[0] }$: ${ entry[1] }$ USD[end]
    [end]
  ' !]

  [Title = id: "live"]Live loops[end:Title]
  [Text]
    When the list is [hl = text: "runtime ${ }$" !], the loop runs in the browser too: it keeps one row per item,
    and the rows follow the list as it changes. Since ArcMoon 1.1:
  [end:Text]
  [Code = output: true, text: '
    runtime ${
      import { signal } from "arcmoon/reactive";
      const todos = signal([{ id: 1, title: "Read a book" }, { id: 2, title: "Call Eid" }]);
      const remove = (id) => todos(todos().filter((t) => t.id !== id));
    }$
    [ul]
      [for-each = runtime ${ todos() }$, as: "todo", key: "id"]
        [li]
          runtime ${ i + 1 }$. runtime ${ todo.title }$
          [button = onclick: runtime ${ () => remove(todo.id) }$]Remove[end]
        [end]
      [end]
    [end]
  ' !]
  [List]
    [Item]The list is a live value, usually a signal. Set a new list to change it: [hl = text: "todos([...todos(), item])" !].[end:Item]
    [Item][hl = text: "todo" !] and [hl = text: "i" !] exist only in [hl = text: "runtime ${ }$" !] inside the loop.[end:Item]
    [Item][hl = text: "key" !] names a property that tells items apart. Rows stay with their items: removing or reordering items moves the real rows, so an input keeps its focus. Without [hl = text: "key" !], rows follow positions.[end:Item]
    [Item]Rows update every time the list is set, even when an item was changed in place. Events always use the row's current item.[end:Item]
  [end:List]

  [Title = size: 3, id: "live-html"]The first rows are in the HTML[end:Title]
  [Text]
    When the list starts as a literal ([hl = text: "signal([…])" !]) or an exported value, ArcMoon writes the first rows into the HTML.
    The list shows before the script runs, and search engines see it. In those rows, a value is filled in when it only needs the item,
    [hl = text: "i" !], exported values and plain JavaScript ([hl = text: "Math" !], [hl = text: "String" !], [hl = text: "JSON" !]…).
    Other values, like another signal or your own function, fill in when the script runs. Events are always added by the script.
  [end:Text]

  [Title = size: 3, id: "live-limits"]Not inside a live loop (yet)[end:Title]
  [Text]These stop the build with a clear message: components, a live loop inside another, [hl = text: "[slot]" !], [hl = text: "[script]" !], and [hl = text: "arcm-ref" !] (it would be on every row; use [hl = text: "arcm-shared-ref" !] and [hl = text: "ArcMoon.refs()" !]). Put the loop inside an element, like [hl = text: "[ul]" !] or [hl = text: "[main]" !].[end:Text]
  [Code = lang: "log", text: '
    ✗ "todo" only exists in the browser, inside a live [for-each]: use runtime ${ todo }$, not ${ todo }$
  ' !]

  [Title = id: "mistakes"]Mistakes[end:Title]
  [Text]With [hl = text: "as" !], [hl = text: "value" !] doesn't exist. Use the name you chose:[end:Text]
  [Code = lang: "log", text: '
    ✗ ReferenceError: value is not defined
  ' !]
[end:Docs]
